# Dave

## Act 1, 03:17

### Who I am

Hello! I am Dave, and I am the one with the scissors. Give me squares and
triangles to cut out and push around the table, and I am happy. If I can
show you a thing, I believe it. I also know that a picture can lie, so I
have promised myself to say out loud everything my pictures lean on. Here
it is.

### The theorem, as I would say it

Take a triangle in the flat plane, with one of its angles a right angle. Call
the lengths of the two sides that meet at the right angle `a` and `b`.
These are the legs. Call the length of the third side, the one across from
the right angle, `c`. This is the hypotenuse. All three are positive
numbers, measured in the same unit. Then

```
a² + b² = c²
```

I like to say it with figures, because that is what the squares in the
formula are: build a square on each of the three sides, and the two squares
on the legs together have exactly the area of the square on the hypotenuse.

```
        |\
        | \
      a |  \ c
        |   \
        |____\
          b
```

Two things are in that statement and are easy to walk past. It is about
*every* right triangle, not one I happened to draw. And it is about the
*flat* plane.

### What I take for granted

My proof will cut a big square into pieces in two ways and compare what is
left. Everything it uses is below. D is a definition, A is an axiom, K is a
known result that I lean on and do not prove here.

**Words**

1. **D. Right triangle, legs, hypotenuse.** As in the statement above.
2. **D. Rectangle and square.** A rectangle is a four-sided figure with
   four right angles. A square is a rectangle with four equal sides.
3. **D. Congruent.** Two figures are congruent when one can be laid exactly
   on the other by sliding, turning or flipping it, with no stretching.
4. **D. Cutting a figure into pieces.** A figure is cut into pieces when
   the pieces together cover all of it and no two of them overlap in more
   than their edges.

**Lengths and angles**

5. **A. Lengths add along a line.** If a point B lies on the segment from A
   to C, then AB + BC = AC.
6. **A. Angles add, and a straight line is two right angles.** Angles side
   by side at one point add up, a right angle is 90°, and the angle of a
   straight line is 180°.
7. **A. A figure can be moved.** Any figure can be slid, turned or flipped
   to anywhere in the plane, and the moved figure has the same lengths and
   the same angles. This is what lets me make four copies of my triangle
   and put them where I want.
8. **K. Side, angle, side.** Two triangles that agree in two sides and the
   angle between them are congruent. So any right triangle with legs `a`
   and `b` is a copy of mine, hypotenuse `c` and all.
9. **K. The angles of a triangle add up to 180°.** So the two angles of my
   triangle that are not the right angle add up to 90°. This is the item
   that makes the plane flat. It rests on the parallel axiom, and I am
   putting it on the table face up, because I think the whole theorem
   hangs on it.
10. **K. Rectangles behave.** A four-sided figure with three right angles
    has a fourth, so it is a rectangle. Opposite sides of a rectangle are
    equal. A square with any given side can be built. A line through a
    rectangle at right angles to one side cuts it into two rectangles.
    These also rest on the parallel axiom.

**Area**

11. **A. Every figure made of straight edges has an area**, a number that
    is not negative.
12. **A. Congruent figures have the same area.** Moving a piece does not
    change how much paper it is.
13. **A. Areas of pieces add.** If a figure is cut into finitely many
    pieces, its area is the sum of theirs.
14. **K. A square with side `s` has area `s²`.** For a side of 3 you
    count nine little squares. For a side like √2 you cannot count, and it
    needs a real argument. I lean on it and I say so. If nobody wants to
    grant it, I can state the theorem about the areas of the three squares
    and not need it at all.

**Numbers**

15. **K. Ordinary arithmetic.** In particular, if equal amounts are taken
    away from equal amounts, what is left is equal.

That is my whole bag. Notice what is not in it: I never need the area of a
triangle or of a rectangle. My four triangles will be the same four in both
pictures, and item 12 is all I need to know about them.

One promise for Act 2. That my pieces really fit, with no gap and no
overlap, and that the hole in the middle really is a square, is **not** on
this list. A drawing makes both look obvious. I will argue them from the
items above.

### What I will not grant without an argument

- **To Bob: the distance formula.** If you write that the distance between
  two points is √((x₂ − x₁)² + (y₂ − y₁)²), you have written down the
  theorem we are trying to prove and called it a definition. I will happily
  grant you axes at right angles and coordinates along them. I will not
  grant you what the length of a slanted segment is until you say where it
  comes from.
- **To Carol: what stretching does to area.** If a figure is scaled by a
  factor `k`, its area is multiplied by `k²`. That is true and it is not
  free. It is very close to item 14, and for a factor that is not a whole
  number it needs the same care. The same goes for "a shear keeps area":
  lovely, and I would like to see why.
- **To anyone, me included: "you can see it in the picture".** A picture is
  where I find a proof. It is not the proof.
- **To Percy, gladly:** if you take me to a globe, my item 9 fails there,
  and I expect the theorem to fail with it. I would not call that a hole in
  the proof. I would call it the reason item 9 is on the list.

## Act 2, 03:28

Scissors out! One note first. The class settled that side-angle-side is an
axiom, so my item 8 is an axiom from here on, and I cite it as "item 8" all
the same.

### 1. The idea

I cut one big square, of side `a + b`, into pieces in two different ways.
Both ways use four copies of my triangle, so what is left over must be the
same amount of paper: one way leaves the square on `c`, the other leaves
the squares on `a` and on `b`.

The two pictures. They stand beside the steps, and no step leans on them.

```
   First cutting                    Second cutting

   P---a---A------b------Q          P---a---A------b------Q
   |      / \            |          |       |           / |
   b    /     \          a          a  a·a  |    a·b  /   |
   |  /         \        |          |       |       /     |
   D              \      B          E-------F-----------G
   | \              \  / |          |     / |             |
   a   \    c·c      /   b          b   /   |     b·b     |
   |     \         /     |          | /a·b  |             |
   S---b---C------a------R          S-------H-------------R
```

### 2. The steps

Throughout, T is my triangle: right angle at its corner, legs `a` and
`b`, hypotenuse `c` (item 1). Call its angle across from the leg `a`
α, and its angle across from the leg `b` β.

**Getting ready**

1. Every figure below has an area, and I write area(X) for it. *Item 11.*
2. α + β = 90°. *Item 9: the three angles of T add up to 180°, and one of
   them is 90° by item 1.*
3. There is a square PQRS with side `a + b`, named going round. Each of
   its four angles is a right angle and each side has length `a + b`.
   *Item 10 says it can be built, item 2 says what a square is.*

**The first cutting**

4. On PQ there is a point A with PA = `a`, and then AQ = `b`. In the
   same way: B on QR with QB = `a` and BR = `b`, C on RS with
   RC = `a` and CS = `b`, D on SP with SD = `a` and DP = `b`.
   *Step 3 for the length of each side, item 5 for the two parts.*
5. The triangle AQB has a right angle at Q, between sides QA = `b` and
   QB = `a`. *Step 3 for the angle, step 4 for the sides.*
6. The triangle AQB is congruent to T. So AB = `c`, its angle at A, which
   lies across from QB = `a`, is α, and its angle at B, across from
   QA = `b`, is β. *Item 8, with step 5 and item 1.*
7. In the same way the triangles BRC, CSD and DPA are congruent to T, so
   BC = CD = DA = `c`, and each of them has the angle β at the end of its
   leg `a` and the angle α at the end of its leg `b`. *Steps 3 and 4
   and item 8, exactly as in steps 5 and 6.*
8. At the point A, the angle of triangle DPA is β and the angle of triangle
   AQB is α. *Step 7: in DPA the corner A is at the end of the leg
   PA = `a`. Step 6 for AQB.*
9. The angle DAB is a right angle. *Item 6: P, A, Q lie on a straight line,
   so the three angles at A on one side of it add up to 180°. Two of them
   are β and α by step 8, and those make 90° by step 2. So the third is
   90°.*
10. In the same way the angles ABC, BCD and CDA are right angles. *Steps 6
    and 7, item 6 and step 2, as in steps 8 and 9.*
11. ABCD is a square with side `c`. *Item 2: four right angles by steps 9
    and 10, four equal sides by steps 6 and 7.*
12. The square PQRS is cut into exactly five pieces: the triangles AQB,
    BRC, CSD, DPA and the figure ABCD. *Item 16, used four times. The
    square is convex. The cut AB joins two points of its edge and takes
    off the triangle AQB, leaving the convex figure PABRS. The cut BC joins
    two points of the edge of that figure and takes off BRC, leaving
    PABCS. The cut CD takes off CSD, leaving PABCD. The cut DA takes off
    DPA, leaving ABCD. Item 4 says this is a cutting into pieces.*
13. area(PQRS) = 4 · area(T) + c². *Item 13 with step 12. Item 12 with
    steps 6 and 7 gives each triangle the area of T. Item 14 with step 11
    gives ABCD the area c².*

**The second cutting, of the same square**

14. On PS there is a point E with PE = `a`, and then ES = `b`. *Step 3
    and item 5.* The point A on PQ is the one from step 4.
15. The line through A at right angles to PQ cuts PQRS into two rectangles,
    PAHS and AQRH, with H on SR. *Item 10.* Then AH = `a + b`,
    SH = `a` and HR = `b`. *Item 10: opposite sides of a rectangle are
    equal. Step 4.*
16. The line through E at right angles to PS cuts PAHS into two rectangles,
    PAFE and EFHS, with F on AH. *Item 10.* The same line goes on and meets
    AH at a right angle at F, so it cuts AQRH into two rectangles as well,
    AQGF and FGRH, with G on QR. *Item 10: PAFE is a rectangle, so its
    angle at F is a right angle, and then item 10 again for AQRH.*
17. The sides are: PAFE is `a` by `a`; FGRH is `b` by `b`; EFHS is
    `a` by `b`; AQGF is `b` by `a`. *Item 10, opposite sides of a
    rectangle, with steps 4, 14 and 15 and item 5.*
18. PAFE is a square with side `a` and FGRH is a square with side `b`.
    *Item 2 with step 17.*
19. The diagonal from S to F of the rectangle EFHS cuts it into two
    triangles, SEF and FHS. Each has a right angle,
    at E and at H, between sides `a` and `b`. *Item 16 for the cut,
    step 16 for the right angles, step 17 for the sides.*
20. Each of those two triangles is congruent to T. *Item 8 with step 19.*
21. In the same way the diagonal from F to Q cuts the rectangle AQGF into
    two triangles, FAQ and QGF, each congruent to T. *Items 16 and 8, with
    steps 16 and 17.*
22. The square PQRS is cut into exactly six pieces: the squares PAFE and
    FGRH and the four triangles of steps 19 and 21. *Steps 15, 16, 19 and
    21, and item 4.*
23. area(PQRS) = a² + b² + 4 · area(T). *Item 13 with step 22. Item 14 with
    step 18. Item 12 with steps 20 and 21.*

**The end**

24. 4 · area(T) + c² = a² + b² + 4 · area(T). *Steps 13 and 23: both are
    the area of the one square PQRS.*
25. c² = a² + b². *Item 15: take 4 · area(T) away from both sides of
    step 24.* And that is the theorem.

### 3. Where the right angle is used

In two places, and they are the two that hold it all up.

- **Step 6 (and 7, 20, 21).** The corner of a square is 90°. The piece I
  cut off there is a copy of T only because T has 90° between its legs
  too. That is where `c` comes into the picture at all.
- **Step 2, used in step 9.** Because one angle of T is 90°, the other two
  make 90°, and that is what makes the hole in the middle square.

For 60° at the corner of T: step 5 still gives a triangle with a right
angle between sides `a` and `b`, since the square's corner has not
changed. But step 6 fails. Item 8 needs the same angle between the two
sides, and T now has 60° there. So the piece cut off is not a copy of T,
its long side AB is not `c`, and `c` never appears. The first cutting
still measures something true, AB² = a² + b², but AB is no longer a side of
my triangle. And the theorem itself is false there, as it should be: with
`a` = `b` = 1 and 60° between them the triangle is equilateral, so
`c` = 1, and a² + b² = 2.

If I try to mend it by using real copies of the 60° triangle, they do not
fit a square corner at all, and step 2 gives α + β = 120°, so the angle in
step 9 comes out as 60° and the middle is not a square.

### 4. Where flatness is used

- **Step 2, item 9.** The angles of a triangle add up to 180°.
- **Steps 3, 15, 16 and 17, item 10.** That a square of a given side
  exists, that a line at right angles to one side of a rectangle meets the
  opposite side at right angles, and that opposite sides of a rectangle are
  equal.

Both items rest on the parallel axiom. On a ball step 3 already fails:
there is no figure there with four right angles.

### 5. Used

1, 2, 4, 5, 6, 8, 9, 10, 11, 12, 13, 14, 15, and the new item 16.

### 6. Not used

- **3**, what congruent means. I only ever get congruence from item 8 and
  spend it through item 12. I never look inside the word.
- **7**, that a figure can be moved. This one made me laugh. I am the one
  who cuts and *moves*, and my proof moves nothing. Every triangle is found
  where it lies, inside the square, and shown to be a copy by item 8. The
  moving in the usual telling is how one finds the two pictures.

**New, missing from my Act 1 list, and I needed it six times:**

16. **K. A straight cut makes two pieces.** A square and a rectangle are
    convex. A straight cut joining two points on the edge of a convex
    figure divides it into exactly two convex figures, one on each side of
    the cut, which overlap only along the cut and together make up the
    whole.

That is the honest price of my Act 1 promise. "The pieces fit" is steps 12
and 22, and every time it is item 16 doing the work. In Act 1 I had the
rule for adding the areas of pieces (item 13) and nothing that says when a
drawing with lines across it really is a cutting into pieces. Without
item 16 steps 12, 19, 21 and 22 would rest on the picture.

One more place where I lean and want it seen: item 14, for `a`, `b` and
`c` that are not whole numbers. It is used three times, in steps 13 and
23, and never for the big square.

### 7. The one thing my proof needs that the others can do without

That a figure can be cut into pieces whose areas add up, items 13 and 16
together: I expect a proof by similar triangles to get `a² + b² = c²`
from ratios of lengths alone and never need to know that any pieces fit.
